<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashikaga Takauji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1305">1305</a>–<a href="/years/1358">1358</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashikaga Takauji was a Japanese warrior who in 1333 turned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a> destroy it. Breaking with Go-Daigo's Kenmu Restoration, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1336, installed an emperor of the rival Northern Court and in 1338 became shogun, founding the Ashikaga, or Muromachi, shogunate. Go-Daigo's Southern Court fought on, beginning a schism of nearly sixty years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji.jpg" alt="Ashikaga Takauji" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashikaga_Takauji_J%C5%8Ddo-ji.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ashikaga Takauji was born on 18 August 1305, and his childhood name was Matagorō. He was a male-line descendant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the Minamoto Seiwa Genji line, who had settled in the Ashikaga area of Shimotsuke Province, in present-day Tochigi Prefecture. His father was Ashikaga Sadauji and his mother Uesugi Kiyoko, and his brother was Ashikaga Tadayoshi. The Zen master Musō Soseki, who enjoyed his favour, said that Takauji kept his cool in battle and was not afraid of death, was merciful and tolerant, and was very generous to those below him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Takauji was a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate, sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1333 to put down the Genkō War, which had begun in 1331. Disillusioned with the shogunate, he joined the banished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Emperor Go-Daigo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusunoki-masashige">Kusunoki Masashige</a> and seized Kyoto. Nitta Yoshisada then besieged Kamakura, and when the city fell the regent Hōjō Takatoki and his clansmen killed themselves, ending the shogunate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>'s power. Go-Daigo was enthroned again and began the Kenmu Restoration.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> grew disillusioned with the restored court, which sought to return to the systems of the Heian period, and Takauji gained favour among those who believed he could restore feudal government. In 1335 he put down a rebellion by Hōjō Tokiyuki and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> for himself, claiming the title of Sei-i Taishōgun and allotting land to his followers without the court's permission.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Go-Daigo</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitta-yoshisada">Nitta Yoshisada</a> against him, and Takauji beat him and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. He held it for a few days in February 1336 before being driven out to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a>. He regained control there and defeated Yoshisada again at the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-minatogawa">Battle of Minatogawa</a> in 1336, killing Masashige, and seized Kyoto for good. He installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-komyo">Emperor Kōmyō</a> of the Northern Court in opposition to the exiled Southern Court, and the Nanbokuchō period began, in which two emperors fought each other for almost sixty years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Takauji was appointed shōgun in 1338, which began the Muromachi period. He spent the last years of his rule in the struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, which Southern forces took and Ashikaga forces retook several times between 1351 and 1358. His brother Tadayoshi, excluded from the administration in 1350, joined the Southern Court in 1351 and died in 1352. Takauji died on 7 June 1358 and was succeeded by his son Ashikaga Yoshiakira. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a> united the Northern and Southern courts in 1392.</p><p>Because he rose by betraying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo">Go-Daigo</a>, Confucian-minded historians have counted him among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s Three Great Villains. The Taiheiki, a 40-volume epic of the Muromachi period, tells the story from the Genkō rebellion to the establishment of the two courts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashikaga Takauji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ashikaga Takauji</b> turned against Emperor Go-Daigo's Kenmu Restoration, took Kyoto in 1336, issued the Kenmu shikimoku as a statement of policy and installed an emperor of the Northern Court, who named him shogun in 1338.</blockquote><a href="/state/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1349 <b>Ashikaga Takauji</b> sent his son Motouji there as Kamakura kubo, assisted by a deputy, the Kanto kanrei, an office soon held by the Uesugi family.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamakura-fu">Kamakura-fu</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Go-Daigo rose against the Hojo, and in 1333 <b>Ashikaga Takauji</b> took Kyoto while Nitta Yoshisada stormed Kamakura, ending the regime and opening the Kenmu Restoration.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate</a></li><li><blockquote>When the warlords <b>Ashikaga Takauji</b> and Nitta Yoshisada turned against the shogunate and destroyed it in 1333, Go-Daigo returned to Kyoto to rule in person, naming his era Kenmu in 1334.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenmu-restoration">Kenmu Restoration</a></li><li><blockquote>When Emperor Go-Daigo broke with <b>Ashikaga Takauji</b>, Takauji placed Komyo, of the rival Jimyoin branch of the imperial house, on the throne in Kyoto in 1336.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-court">Northern Court</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kamakura, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Kyōto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.32,139.55&amp;year=1339" aria-label="Kamakura on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8081 887 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ashikaga Takauji"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8531 1157h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8531 1157h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.32,139.55&amp;year=1339">What was here in 1339? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
